03 — Wazuh Agent Deployment and Brute-Force Detection

**Goal:** Deploy a Wazuh agent on a monitored host and confirm it generates real alerts for suspicious authentication activity.

**Setup:** Wazuh manager (10.10.10.5) and a fresh Rocky Linux 10 host, `rocky-target` (10.10.10.6), both on the isolated LabNet (10.10.10.0/24). Attempts were made from Kali (10.10.10.3).

**Steps taken:**

```
rpm --import https://packages.wazuh.com/key/GPG-KEY-WAZUH

tee /etc/yum.repos.d/wazuh.repo << 'EOF'
[wazuh]
gpgcheck=1
gpgkey=https://packages.wazuh.com/key/GPG-KEY-WAZUH
enabled=1
name=EL-\$releasever - Wazuh
baseurl=https://packages.wazuh.com/4.x/yum/
protect=1
EOF

WAZUH_MANAGER='10.10.10.5' dnf install -y wazuh-agent
systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl enable wazuh-agent
systemctl start wazuh-agent
```

Confirmed the agent showed **Active** in the Wazuh dashboard under Agents.

Then, from Kali, attempted several SSH logins to `rocky-target` with an intentionally wrong password:

```
ssh Senio@10.10.10.6
```

https://github.com/Senio219/cyber-lab-writeups/blob/5ee6402d2750ecf520e5679110e97d0d1cdf68a0/Screenshot%202026-11-26%20023254.png

**Result:** Wazuh generated multiple correlated alerts within seconds:
- `sshd: authentication failed`
- `unix_chkpwd: Password check failed`
- `PAM: User login failed`
- `syslog: User missed the password` (a higher-level summary alert)



